From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 02:35:18 -0400
Subject: feat: Cmd-N's Space, the bookmark bar's way back, and a question
 before a big conversion

The three decisions left open for the owner, made on the night of
2026-10-22 with the owner away and asking for them to be decided:

- Cmd-N opens on the Space of the window it is pressed in (windows G6;
  BACKLOG S-145), as a window a tab is dragged into or moved to already
  did. A window made with no window to come from, as the Dock's New Window
  with none open, keeps the profile's first Space. The Space rides from
  NewWindow to the new window's SpaceModel through
  SpaceModel::SetNextWindowSpace, cleared once the window is made.
- Settings > Stedding > Tabs > "Show the bookmark bar and the star"
  (settings T27, import I14; docs/history/plan-2026-09-26.md WIN-15, its
  option B): the inverse of stedding.bookmarks.converted, off after Turn
  Bookmarks into Pinned Tabs; turned on, the bar and the star come back in
  every window at once, since both already watch the preference. Option A,
  hiding the bar once through Chromium's own setting, would have undone
  the sidebar standing for the bookmark bar.
- Turn Bookmarks into Pinned Tabs, from the command bar, asks first above
  50 bookmarks (import I16; WEB-11): a dialog over the window names how
  many it would add, not counting those an earlier run made pins of, with
  Turn into Pinned Tabs and Cancel. Up to 50 it converts at once, as
  before.

Tests: SpaceSwitchTest.CmdNOpensOnTheSpaceItIsPressedIn;
CommandBarActionsTest.TheSettingsRowBringsTheBarAndTheStarBack and
CommandBarActionsTest.ManyBookmarksAskBeforeTurningIntoPins.

Why: the owner left these three for a decision and, away for the night,
asked for them to be decided; each also closes a way a user got stuck (no
way back to the bookmark bar, hundreds of tabs from one command, Cmd-N
leaving the Space in use).
Removable when: never; the next re-cut folds each into the patch that owns
its feature (windows, import, settings).
